---
/**
 * Every floor, ceiling and clamp in the modelled formula, and who is standing on each one.
 *
 * # Why this is a route rather than a card
 *
 * The site's other drawings sit under the thing they are about: a chart of base cost goes on the
 * base cost component, a chart of the guarantee on the guarantee. The census of the plan's bounds
 * is about the *whole* plan — thirty-eight edges at once, across five parts of R.C. 3317 — and
 * filing it under any one of them would be filing a map of a country under one of its provinces.
 * So it is a page, and `corpusSeries.ts`'s `PAGE_SERIES` is the small table that says a series can
 * be drawn by one, with the plan's own corpus node still answering for the figures at its ends.
 *
 * It sits in `Research` beside Outcomes, History, Scenario and Reach. Those four ask what the
 * formula did to somebody. This one asks what shape the formula is, which is the question a reader
 * has to have answered before any of the other four means very much.
 *
 * # The layout is the site's card stack, not `/reach`'s stage
 *
 * `/reach` gives its picture the whole frame because the picture is interactive and the prose is
 * apparatus around it. This chart does not move: it is one of a series of findings a reader works
 * down, and the two null results at the bottom are as much of the answer as the ranking at the
 * top. So it is cards, in order, like every other static page here.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SectionAnchor from "../components/SectionAnchor.astro";
import {
  census,
  renderCensus,
  renderFamilies,
  renderNulls,
  renderTable,
} from "../lib/bounds.ts";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count } from "../lib/format.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";

const { bundle } = loadFeed();
const c = census();
---

<Base
  title="Bounds"
  description={`Every floor, ceiling and clamp in Ohio's school funding formula — ${count(c.rows.length)} of them — ranked by how many of the state's districts each one is actually the operative term for, with the one that cannot bind for any district at all.`}
  image={routes.og.page("bounds")}
  imageAlt="Every bound in Ohio's school funding formula, ranked by the number of districts it is the operative term for"
  section="bounds"
>
  <h1>Bounds</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Ohio's funding formula is written in floors and ceilings: a district is funded for at least one
    EMIS employee, its capacity percentage is capped, its guarantee cannot fall below zero. There
    are {count(c.rows.length)} such bounds in the model, and for any given district most of them do
    nothing at all. This is the census of which ones actually decide an amount, and for how many of
    the state's {count(c.whole)} districts.
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={renderCensus(c)} />

  {/*
    The apparatus, under the thing it is apparatus for — `/reach`'s ordering, and for its reason.
    A reader arriving here can read the picture before the definition; a reader who wants the
    definition has not had to scroll past three paragraphs to reach the picture.
  */}
  <div class="card" id="what-this-is" data-part="what-this-is">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="what-this-is" />What counts as a bound, and what "operative" means</h2>
    <p class="note">
      A <strong>bound</strong> here is a place where the statute takes a maximum or a minimum — a
      floor, a ceiling, or a clamp to a range — rather than computing straight through. R.C.
      3317.011(F)(5)(b) funds "one EMIS support employee, or the district's enrolled ADM divided by
      one thousand, whichever is greater": that <em>whichever is greater</em> is one bound.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      A bound is <strong>operative</strong> for a district when it is the side that wins. For a
      district with 400 pupils the EMIS floor is operative, because one employee is more than 0.4;
      for a district with 4,000 it is not, and the bound is present in the arithmetic and decides
      nothing. <strong>The count beside each bound is how many districts it decides something
      for</strong>, and it is not a measure of how much money moves — a bound can be operative for
      five hundred districts and be worth a few thousand dollars in each.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      This is the FY{bundle.fiscal_year} model as this repository runs it, over{" "}
      {count(c.whole)} districts. It counts the side a provision <em>produces</em>, so a district
      exactly on a bound is counted as being on it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Fragment set:html={renderFamilies(c)} />
  <Fragment set:html={renderNulls(c)} />
  <Fragment set:html={renderTable(c)} />

  <div class="card" id="not-here" data-part="not-here">
    <h2><SectionAnchor id="not-here" />What this census does not count</h2>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Steps and brackets are not bounds.</strong> A schedule that pays one rate below a
      threshold and another above it is a piecewise function, not a maximum, and a district is
      always in exactly one of its pieces. The capacity tiers of targeted assistance are the
      largest example: they are in the formula, they change what a district is paid, and no row
      above names them.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Prorations are not bounds either.</strong> Where an appropriation is insufficient the
      statute scales every payment by a common factor; that is a multiplier applied to everyone,
      not a term that wins for some districts and loses for others.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>And a count of districts is not a count of dollars.</strong> The ranking above says
      how often a bound is the deciding term, not what turns on it. The one place where those two
      come apart most sharply is the guarantee, which is operative for roughly half the state and
      carries about an eighth of the money — see <a href={routes.REACH}>Reach</a> for who it holds
      still, and <a href="/scenario">Scenario</a> for what moving a lever costs.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
